        "root_concepts": "[nuclear, deterrence]",
        "section": "political-science",
        "source_custody": "partial",
        "status": "source-custody",
        "title": "Nuclear Deterrence",
        "updated": "null"
      },
      "text": "**Formal bridge:** If you have the TCS/formal background, you already own the\nmachinery — here's the domain mapping. MAD is a Nash equilibrium in a simultaneous\ngame with catastrophic payoffs: both sides choose \"don't strike first\" because\nthe payoff of striking first (annihilation from second strike) is worse than\nmutual restraint, assuming secure second-strike capability. The credibility problem\nis the cheap-talk vs. costly-signaling distinction: verbal threats are cheap talk\n(no cost to make them, adversary can't verify resolve), so they are not credible\nsignals. Schelling's commitment devices — trip wires, hostage forces, delegated\nlaunch authority — are costly signals that make retaliation automatic or\nmechanism-enforced, removing the cheap-talk problem. The positive/negative control\ntension is a classic principal-agent problem under moral hazard: you want the agent\n(launch system) to act only when the principal (national command authority) authorizes,\nbut you also want the threat to be credible, which requires the agent to be able to\nact without principal approval in some scenarios."
    },
    {
      "id": "02-NUCLEAR-DETERRENCE.md#004",
      "path": [
        "02-NUCLEAR-DETERRENCE.md"
